window.__DATA=window.__DATA||{};window.__DATA['bank/2025_06_3']={"id":"2025_06_3","title":"2025年6月 第3套","year":2025,"month":6,"set":"3","readingOnly":false,"hasAudio":true,"audio":"audio/4b2ef312.mp3","audioDuration":1425.8,"audioMissing":false,"counts":{"total":55,"listening":25,"cloze":10,"matching":10,"reading":10},"passages":{"passage1":{"kind":"passage","text":"Our society places a high value on physical beauty. Americans spend an average of over $722 each year on their appearance. One in ten Americans has tried to look like a star. There’s nothing wrong with trying to look our best, but excessive focus on physical appearance misses the soulful aspects of what it means to be beautiful. Trying to look like the magazine pictures can take us on a long ride away from what beauty is really about. Many of us spend far too much time, energy, and money trying to polish an image of what we think will bring attention, love, and connection. In the process, we may fail to attend to ourselves in a way that would move us toward deeper intimacy, fulfillment, and meaning. It may sound trite (老生常谈的), but beauty is only skin deep; it’s not what brings love toward us. Just read about the latest Hollywood starlets whose seeming love turns into resentment and bitter court battles. Outer beauty can be as much of a curse as a blessing. We may develop a habit of being so focused on maintaining a perfect appearance that we never cultivate the inner qualities necessary to sustain and deepen intimacy and connection. As I describe in my book, The Authentic Heart, it is the courage to be authentic that connects us in a deeper way. While our initial attraction may be based, in part, on physical chemistry, it is the meeting of our inner worlds that creates the lasting intimacy and spiritual connection for which we long. If we redirect our attention toward cultivating inner qualities, we might find a magnetic attraction that moves us from something superficial to something that connects us to our depths. The path toward cultivating inner beauty is really simple. But what is simple is not always easy. Not everyone will see us and appreciate us as we take the courageous risk to allow ourselves to be seen as we really are. But if they don’t, it is their loss, not ours. Eventually we’ll find those compatible souls who appreciate us as we are."},"passage2":{"kind":"passage","text":"Plant-based meats are coming soon to a dinner table near you, but do they deserve a spot on your plate? If you tried a vegetable burger years ago and dismissed it as rubbery and flavorless, it’s a good time to give meatless meat another chance. Newer varieties imitate the look, flavor, and texture of meat. Analysts don’t expect the meat-free movement to slow anytime soon; a 2021 report predicts the market will skyrocket to $74 billion by 2030—a 957 percent surge. Plant-based meats are no longer just for vegetarians. Companies such as Impossible Foods and Beyond Meat—the current superstars of the alternative protein sector—are pursuing consumers who enjoy meat but want to reduce their meat consumption for animal rights, health, or environmental reasons. So far, it’s a winning strategy: More than nine out of ten consumers who buy Beyond Burger and Impossible Foods products also eat meat. Many vegetarians don’t actually care for food that resembles meat. When reporter Joan Solsman, a longtime vegetarian, tried a dish from Impossible Foods, she felt so sick that she couldn’t finish it. “Maybe the best sign that Impossible Foods has cracked the code to realistic fake meat is that I couldn’t stand to take another bite,” she wrote. Meatless meats are generally a healthier choice than beef because they are cholesterol-free, plus they’re a good source of vitamins, minerals, protein, and fiber. If meatless meat can help you stick to a plant-based diet, that in itself can lead to better health. Not only has red meat been linked to cancer, but studies show that people who ditch meat have lower blood pressure, lower average blood sugar, and lower cholesterol levels. However, just because it’s plant-based doesn’t mean it’s health food. Most imitation meats are highly processed and contain high amounts of sodium (钠) compared to traditional beef. These new generations of alternate meat get all the attention, but don’t forget about whole grains and vegetables. Beans are an especially excellent protein substitute. They’re nutritious, inexpensive, and far more sustainable than any of the processed meatless substitutes on the market today."},"cloze":{"kind":"cloze","text":"with a single line through the centre. You may not use any of the words in the bank more than once. The human brain has the ability to constantly form new networks and connections between brain cells. This means the brain has an almost magical ability to change. This characteristic is 26 apparent in a new study, focusing on people who underwent a hemispherectomy, a surgical procedure in which half of the brain is removed to treat illness. The findings showed that, despite missing half of this critical 27 , participants could function well. This was because the 28 half of their brain strengthened. In fact, researchers found that connections—and thus communication—between parts of different brain networks are actually stronger in people who had hemispherectomies than in people with intact brains. This 29 that the brain is able to compensate for loss of certain brain structure. There are many other cases in the medical 30 that document the brain’s amazing ability to 31 . For example, a young boy had a significant portion of his brain removed for medical reasons, which included the part of the brain 32 for sight. But a few years after his surgery, scientists found that he could 33 see. They determined that this was because other parts of his brain took over the missing part’s 34 tasks. Another study involved people who could smell, despite missing the region in the brain that processes information about smells. Though they are not sure how these individuals 35 the ability to smell, scientists believe it is possible that another part of the brain took on that task.","wordBank":{"A":"adapt","B":"hardly","C":"indicates","D":"insist","E":"literature","F":"organ","G":"particularly","H":"remaining","I":"responsible","J":"retained","K":"still","L":"strategy","M":"transformed","N":"underlines","O":"visual"}},"matching":{"kind":"matching","text":"","paragraphs":{"A":"Traffic jams are a common problem of most big cities, either in developed or in developing countries, but one kind of traffic jams in particular can be especially frustrating. These are generally known as “phantom” traffic jams, in which dense traffic crawls to a halt for no apparent reason at all.","B":"“When caught in such a traffic jam, it is common for drivers to assume that there must be an accident, or construction or something,” said Massachusetts Institute of Technology (MIT) computer science professor Berthold Horn. “And then you drive and drive and drive… and there isn’t anything there.”","C":"The exact cause of these phantom traffic jams has long remained a mystery. But now, new research has found the cause and researchers may have come up with a solution for this frustrating traffic pattern: maintaining equal spacing between cars on the road, rather than driving so closely behind another vehicle that a collision (碰撞) is likely if that vehicle brakes suddenly.","D":"Horn said phantom traffic jams are an emergent property of the flow of vehicles down a highway. A phantom jam begins when a car in dense traffic slows down even slightly, which causes the car behind that vehicle to slow even more—and the slowing action spreads backward through the lane of traffic like a wave, getting worse the farther it spreads. Eventually, the cars far behind are forced to stop completely or risk hitting the slower vehicles ahead—and so the traffic grinds to a halt over nothing, Horn said.","E":"Horn has been working on the problem of phantom traffic jams for years, and he has come up with a solution: By splitting the difference between the cars in front and the cars behind, the spacing of cars in a stream of traffic can act as a damper (抑制) on phantom traffic jams. That is, it can prevent the slow-down effect from being amplified (增强) by the cars that follow behind, he said. In essence, each driver would constantly make adjustments to keep their car roughly halfway between the car in front and the car behind.","F":"Horn calls this method of vehicle spacing bilateral (双边的) control, and he said it could be achieved with relatively simple modifications to the adaptive cruise control that many new cars already have. Adaptive cruise control is a driver assistance technology that sets a maximum speed for vehicles and automatically slows the speed of the car when traffic is sensed in front of the vehicle. The technology is also known as autonomous cruise control. Such modifications, Horn said, could help fight phantom jams within a few years, maybe decades before an alternative solution, such as networked self-driving cars, becomes a reality on the roads.","G":"Traffic jams are caused by a number of factors, such as roads that are poorly designed or over-capacity, localized factors like city traffic-lighting patterns, and events like accidents and construction. But phantom traffic jams can make all those problems worse, Horn said. And the researchers in the new study have calculated that solving phantom jams could save at least some of the estimated $121 billion per year that traffic jams are estimated to cost the U.S. economy, Horn told Live Science.","H":"New research by Horn and MIT postdoctoral associate Liang Wang, published in the journal IEEE Transactions on Intelligent Transportation Systems, shows how bilateral control can fight the problem. The researchers made computer models of real-world traffic conditions, showing how a typical U.S. freeway could benefit from cars using bilateral control. That would be in place when human drivers fail to slow down, thus avoiding tailgating (紧跟) the car in front, Horn said. “Under reasonable conditions today, you might get 1,800 cars per lane per hour throughput (the volume of traffic passing through),” he added. “But with bilateral control, you could almost double that.”","I":"The main alternative to increasing traffic throughput per lane has been to build more lanes and more highways, and putting in new roads is very expensive, he said. “If we can increase the throughput on major highways, even if it’s only by 50 percent, that would be a big deal,” Horn said.","J":"With the help of engineering students at high schools in Maine, Horn and Wang have also built a robot simulation ( 模 拟 ) that shows how bilateral control can suppress phantom traffic jams. Increasing the spacing of vehicles dampens the effect of slowdowns by cars in front of a given car, so that the slowing is not worsened until it becomes a phantom jam, the researchers said.","K":"A video from the project shows a train of autonomous robots running along a track without bilateral control; they eventually back up in phantom jams. But when the robots are switched over to bilateral control (by flashing the lights in the room), each autonomous robot tries to keep itself halfway between the robot in front and the one behind; the phantom jams disappear as a result, Horn said.","L":"Since starting his research on phantom traffic jams, Horn has learned that schools of fish and flocks of birds and bats use similar spacing strategies to avoid hitting each other while swimming or flying in densely packed groups. In particular, studies of millions of bats emerging at dusk from caves in Southeast Asia showed that each bat used a form of bilateral control to reduce the likelihood of collisions with other bats in the densely packed swarm (一大群), Horn said. M) Unlike bats, birds and fish, however, humans have difficulty judging distances behind them. To address that weakness, Horn is now working with the car company Toyota to enable a form of bilateral control via modifications to existing adaptive cruise control systems, which use forward-facing sensors to judge the distance to the vehicle ahead. The bilateral control approach would include adding rear-facing sensors to determine the distance to the vehicle following a car. N) But until those systems become standard, human drivers can help reduce phantom traffic jams simply by not following the car in front so closely. As Horn argues, “There’s absolutely no advantage of tailgating the car in front of you.”"},"letters":["A","B","C","D","E","F","G","H","I","J","K","L"]}},"questions":[{"no":1,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"1","options":{"A":"They are young teenagers from Atlanta.","B":"They won this year’s 7 Mile Bridge Run.","C":"They completed the race in less than 40 minutes.","D":"They are both students at Marathon High School."},"script":"[1] A Florida teenager and an Atlanta woman won the overall men's and women's divisions Saturday at the annual\n7 Mile Bridge Run. Vaclav Bursa, 15, of Big Pine Key, Florida, finished first overall.He completed the race in 41\nminutes and 1 second and claimed the top spot in the men's division. Bursa won third place in the 2022 race. He is a\nstudent at Marathon High School. Joanna Stephens, 28, of Atlanta, completed the course in 41 minutes and 12 seconds.\nShe won the women's division title and placed second overall. The race drew competitors from 41 US states. There\nwere also runners from other countries, including Canada, the UK and Switzerland. The run closed the span between\nthe Middle and Lower Keys to traffic for 3 hours Saturday. [2] The April contest is one of the most popular running\nevents in the southeastern United States. The race field filled within minutes after online registration opened in January.","audioStart":43.2,"groupNote":"Questions 1 to 2","answer":"B","expl":"What does the news report say about Vaclav Bursa and Joanna Stephens? 关于瓦茨拉夫·布尔萨和乔安娜·斯蒂芬斯，新闻报道说了什么？\nA）他们是来自亚特兰大的低龄青少年。\nB）他们赢得了今年的七英里大桥赛跑。 新闻开头提到，周六，在一年一度的七英里大桥赛跑中，一名佛罗里达州青少年和一名亚特兰大女子分别 赢得了男子组和女子组的冠军，故选项B为正确答案，同时可排除选项A。根据新闻中的细节描述可知， 男子组冠军瓦茨拉夫·布尔萨的比赛成绩为41分1秒，女子组冠军乔安娜·斯蒂芬斯的比赛成绩为41分12 秒，两人用时均超过40分钟，故排除选项C。新闻中只提到瓦茨拉夫·布尔萨是马拉松高中的学生，而28 岁的乔安娜·斯蒂芬斯显然不可能是马拉松高中的在读生，故排除选项D。\nC）他们用时不到40分钟完成了比赛。\nD）他们都是马拉松高中的学生。"},{"no":2,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"2","options":{"A":"It drew competitors from more than 40 countries.","B":"It is watched live online by fans all over the world.","C":"It closed the city of Big Pine Key to traffic for three hours.","D":"It is one of the most popular running events in the southeastern U.S."},"script":"[1] A Florida teenager and an Atlanta woman won the overall men's and women's divisions Saturday at the annual\n7 Mile Bridge Run. Vaclav Bursa, 15, of Big Pine Key, Florida, finished first overall.He completed the race in 41\nminutes and 1 second and claimed the top spot in the men's division. Bursa won third place in the 2022 race. He is a\nstudent at Marathon High School. Joanna Stephens, 28, of Atlanta, completed the course in 41 minutes and 12 seconds.\nShe won the women's division title and placed second overall. The race drew competitors from 41 US states. There\nwere also runners from other countries, including Canada, the UK and Switzerland. The run closed the span between\nthe Middle and Lower Keys to traffic for 3 hours Saturday. [2] The April contest is one of the most popular running\nevents in the southeastern United States. The race field filled within minutes after online registration opened in January.","audioStart":43.2,"groupNote":"Questions 1 to 2","answer":"D","expl":"关于四月的这场比赛，我们可以得知什么？\nA）它吸引了来自40多个国家的参赛者。\nB）它可以通过网络让全世界的粉丝实时观看。\nC）它导致大松礁岛的交通封闭了三个小时。\nD）它是美国东南部最受欢迎的跑步赛事之一。 四级2025.6二号21 新闻结尾部分提到，四月的这场比赛是美国东南部最受欢迎的跑步赛事之一，故选项D为正确答案。新闻 中提到，此次比赛吸引了美国41个州的参赛者，而非41个国家，故排除选项A。比赛时，中礁岛与下礁岛 之间的交通封闭，而非大松岛的交通封闭，故排除选项C。选项B在新闻中并未提及，故可排除。 News ReportTwo"},{"no":3,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"3","options":{"A":"He was once a full-time ambassador.","B":"He was soon to retire from the army.","C":"He became astronaut in 2015.","D":"He used to be a helicopter pilot."},"script":"British astronaut Tim Peake is stepping down permanently from his role as a European astronaut. He's going to take up a full-time position as ambassador for science and space instead—work he's been doing since 2019. [3] The former British Army Air Corps helicopter pilot was selected as a European Space Agency astronaut in 2009. He flew\nto the space station for a 6-month tour in 2015. The tour saw him control a robot on earth remotely from orbit. He also\nhelped dock two spacecraft. Tim said, \"Being an astronaut has been the most extraordinary experience, I've had the\nprivilege of working with an exceptional team of individuals during the past 13 years with the agency- [4] It has been\nincredibly exciting and rewarding. By assuming the role of an ambassador for human flights to space, I'll continue to\nsupport the European and the UK space agencies.\"","audioStart":168.6,"groupNote":"Questions 3 to 4","answer":"D","expl":"关于蒂姆·皮克，我们可以得知什么？\nA）他曾经是一名全职大使。\nB）他很快将从军队退役。\nC）他在2015年成为一名宇航员。\nD）他曾经是一名直升机飞行员。 本篇新闻全程在介绍宇航员蒂姆·皮克，新闻中间部分提到，这位英国陆军航空兵团前直升机飞行员于 2009年被选为欧洲宇航局的宇航员。由此可知，选项D为正确答案，同时排除选项C。其余两项新闻中均 未提及，可排除。"},{"no":4,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"4","options":{"A":"It has been unbelievably exciting and rewarding.","B":"It has been extraordinarily tough and demanding.","C":"It has contributed a great deal to human flights to space.","D":"It has given him exceptional privilege as an ambassador."},"script":"British astronaut Tim Peake is stepping down permanently from his role as a European astronaut. He's going to take up a full-time position as ambassador for science and space instead—work he's been doing since 2019. [3] The former British Army Air Corps helicopter pilot was selected as a European Space Agency astronaut in 2009. He flew\nto the space station for a 6-month tour in 2015. The tour saw him control a robot on earth remotely from orbit. He also\nhelped dock two spacecraft. Tim said, \"Being an astronaut has been the most extraordinary experience, I've had the\nprivilege of working with an exceptional team of individuals during the past 13 years with the agency- [4] It has been\nincredibly exciting and rewarding. By assuming the role of an ambassador for human flights to space, I'll continue to\nsupport the European and the UK space agencies.\"","audioStart":168.6,"groupNote":"Questions 3 to 4","answer":"A","expl":"What does Tim Peake say about his work with the European Space Agency? 关于在欧洲宇航局的工作，蒂姆·皮克说了什么？\nA）它是极其令人振奋且有意义的。\nB）它极其艰难且要求极高。\nC）它对人类的太空飞行做出了巨大贡献。\nD）它赋予了他作为大使的罕见特权。 解新闻后半部分蒂姆评价自己在欧洲宇航局13年的工作时说，这是一段极其令人振奋且有意义的经历。由此 可知，选项A为正确答案。其余三项新闻中均未提及，可排除。 News ReportThree"},{"no":5,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"5","options":{"A":"They are known for their loyalty to their owners.","B":"They are brought in by the store’s customers.","C":"They are regular visitors to the store.","D":"They are pets kept by the store staff."},"script":"[5] An Asda store in Wales is now home to five cats after they wandered in and made themselves comfortable\nThe supermarket is known for its loyal customers, but now has a different kind of visitor that has appeared over the\nlast year. Regular shoppers at the supermarket are familiar with the sight of several cats settling down in the shop\naisles. The unusual situation started about 12 months ago, when one cat routinely started visiting the store. [6] Local\nnewspapers reported that puzzled customers posted on Facebook groups to try to find out if the cat was lost. But its\nowner assured the concerned customers it was very much loved and fed at home. It just enjoyed visiting the shop. Staff\ndid their best to discourage the pet from hanging around. But instead of leaving and never coming back, it invited its\nfriends round, [5] There are now at least five different cats who come almost on a daily basis. They visit the store so\nmuch that [7] staff have been forced to put up signs, asking their customers not to feed them.","audioStart":285.6,"groupNote":"Questions 5 to 7","answer":"C","expl":"What do we learn about the five cats seen in the Asda store? 关于在阿斯达超市看到的那五只猫，我们可以得知什么？ 22四版2025.6第二#\nA）它们以对主人的忠诚而闻名。\nB）它们是由商店的顾客带进来的。 新闻开头就提到，威尔士的一家阿斯达超市如今成了五只猫的家。新闻末尾部分也提到，现在至少有五只 不同的猫儿乎每天都来超市，非常频繁地光顾超市。由此可知，选项C为正确答案。其余三个选项新闻中 均未提及，可排除。\nC）它们是商店的常客。\nD）它们是商店员工养的宠物。"},{"no":6,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"6","options":{"A":"It might be a nuisance to the loyal shoppers.","B":"It might have been abandoned by its owner.","C":"It might be 12 months old.","D":"It might have got lost."},"script":"[5] An Asda store in Wales is now home to five cats after they wandered in and made themselves comfortable\nThe supermarket is known for its loyal customers, but now has a different kind of visitor that has appeared over the\nlast year. Regular shoppers at the supermarket are familiar with the sight of several cats settling down in the shop\naisles. The unusual situation started about 12 months ago, when one cat routinely started visiting the store. [6] Local\nnewspapers reported that puzzled customers posted on Facebook groups to try to find out if the cat was lost. But its\nowner assured the concerned customers it was very much loved and fed at home. It just enjoyed visiting the shop. Staff\ndid their best to discourage the pet from hanging around. But instead of leaving and never coming back, it invited its\nfriends round, [5] There are now at least five different cats who come almost on a daily basis. They visit the store so\nmuch that [7] staff have been forced to put up signs, asking their customers not to feed them.","audioStart":285.6,"groupNote":"Questions 5 to 7","answer":"D","expl":"What did some customers think when they saw the first cat routinely appear in the store? 当看见第一只猫定期出现在超市里时，一些顾客是怎么想的？\nA）它可能对忠实的购物者造成麻烦。\nB）它可能被主人遗弃了。 解一新闻中提到，12个月前，有一只猫开始定期光顾这家超市，一头雾水的顾客在脸书群组中发帖，试图弄清 楚这只猫是否迷路了。由此可知，选项D为正确答案。其余三个选项新闻中均未提及，可排除。\nC）它可能有12个月大。\nD）它可能迷路了。"},{"no":7,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"7","options":{"A":"Feed the cats.","B":"Remove any store signs.","C":"Sit in the shop aisles.","D":"Bring in pets with them."},"script":"[5] An Asda store in Wales is now home to five cats after they wandered in and made themselves comfortable\nThe supermarket is known for its loyal customers, but now has a different kind of visitor that has appeared over the\nlast year. Regular shoppers at the supermarket are familiar with the sight of several cats settling down in the shop\naisles. The unusual situation started about 12 months ago, when one cat routinely started visiting the store. [6] Local\nnewspapers reported that puzzled customers posted on Facebook groups to try to find out if the cat was lost. But its\nowner assured the concerned customers it was very much loved and fed at home. It just enjoyed visiting the shop. Staff\ndid their best to discourage the pet from hanging around. But instead of leaving and never coming back, it invited its\nfriends round, [5] There are now at least five different cats who come almost on a daily basis. They visit the store so\nmuch that [7] staff have been forced to put up signs, asking their customers not to feed them.","audioStart":285.6,"groupNote":"Questions 5 to 7","answer":"A","expl":"顾客们被要求不要做什么？\nA）喂猫。\nB）移除任何商店标识 新闻未尾提到，五只猫每天都来超市，如此频繁地光顾超市，以至于员工们不得不张贴告示，要求顾客们 不要投喂它们。故选项A为正确答案。其余三个选项新闻中均未提及，可排除。 SectionB Conversation One\nC）坐在商店的过道里。\nD）带宠物进来。"},{"no":8,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"8","options":{"A":"Sharing her room with friends.","B":"Cutting down her living expenses.","C":"Living off campus.","D":"Moving to the city center."},"script":"W: [8] I've been thinking about moving off campus next semester.\nM: Hmm... I don't know if that's a good idea as your mother and I would worry about you living all by yourself in an\napartment so far from home. [9] And then there's the expense, too. I'm not sure we can afford it.\nW: Well, I wouldn't be alone, actually, Three of my friends from the dormitory would share the place with me. And\nwith four of us, it wouldn't cost any more than living in the dorm.\nM: Are you sure about that? [9] Rents in big cities are high, and then there's the cost of electricity and gas. Have you\nfactored those in?\nW: We've budgeted for those, and for Internet and transportation. And if we move far enough from the city center, the\nrent is low enough for us to afford all of it.\nM: What's about the commute to and from campus? It sounds like you'll be wasting several hours every day on the\nroad instead of studying.\nW: [ioj It's about an hour to campus, but there's no reason I can't study on the bus. After all, I did that during high\nschool and my commute was even longer then.\nM: That's true, But I'm not sure why you want to leave campus and go through all that trouble when the cost is the\nsame.\nW: For a lot of reasons. We'd have a kitchen, so we could cook ourselves. And we'd each have our own bedroom,\nwhere we could study instead of going to the library. We just have more space and privacy.\nM: [li] I'm not convinced that the benefits are greater than the disadvantages, but I'll discuss this with your mother.","audioStart":464.7,"groupNote":"Questions 8 to 11","answer":"C","expl":"女士一直在考虑下学期做什么？\nA）和朋友合住她的房间。\nB）削减她的生活开支。 四级2025.6 23 在对话开头，女土就开门见山地提出自已一直在考忠下学期搬出校园居住，所以答案是C项。对话中女土 表示搬出校园后她将和宿舍的三个朋友合租公寓，每人均有自已的卧室，而非与朋友合住同一间房，故排 除A项：对话中女土提到，四个人分推的话，住在校外的费用不会比住宿舍高，这说明削减生活开支并不 是女士的目的，故排除B项：对话中女士提到，如果搬到离市中心足够远的地方住，费用将足够便宜，D 项与此完全相反，显然不在女士考虑之中，故也排除。\nC）住在校外。\nD）搬到市中心。"},{"no":9,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"9","options":{"A":"The facilities available.","B":"Various costs involved.","C":"Changes in living conditions.","D":"The distance to her home."},"script":"W: [8] I've been thinking about moving off campus next semester.\nM: Hmm... I don't know if that's a good idea as your mother and I would worry about you living all by yourself in an\napartment so far from home. [9] And then there's the expense, too. I'm not sure we can afford it.\nW: Well, I wouldn't be alone, actually, Three of my friends from the dormitory would share the place with me. And\nwith four of us, it wouldn't cost any more than living in the dorm.\nM: Are you sure about that? [9] Rents in big cities are high, and then there's the cost of electricity and gas. Have you\nfactored those in?\nW: We've budgeted for those, and for Internet and transportation. And if we move far enough from the city center, the\nrent is low enough for us to afford all of it.\nM: What's about the commute to and from campus? It sounds like you'll be wasting several hours every day on the\nroad instead of studying.\nW: [ioj It's about an hour to campus, but there's no reason I can't study on the bus. After all, I did that during high\nschool and my commute was even longer then.\nM: That's true, But I'm not sure why you want to leave campus and go through all that trouble when the cost is the\nsame.\nW: For a lot of reasons. We'd have a kitchen, so we could cook ourselves. And we'd each have our own bedroom,\nwhere we could study instead of going to the library. We just have more space and privacy.\nM: [li] I'm not convinced that the benefits are greater than the disadvantages, but I'll discuss this with your mother.","audioStart":464.7,"groupNote":"Questions 8 to 11","answer":"B","expl":"男士建议女士考虑什么？\nA）可用的设施。\nB）涉及的各种费用。\nC）生活条件的变化。\nD）到她家的距离。 解对话中，男士听说女土打算搬出校园居住后他提到了费用问题，说不确定自已和女士的妈妈能否负担得 起，后面又明确提到大城市的房租很高，还有电费和煤气费，问女土是否把这些因素都考虑在内，他使用 了factor.in这一固定短语，意为“把考虑在内”，这与问题中出现的固定短语take..intoconsideration （把考虑在内）属于同义转述关系，故答案为B项。A项在对话中没有提到，故排除：虽然女土提到 了搬出去住后生活条件的一些变化，但这并非男士建议女士考虑的内容，故排除C项：男士担心的是女士 上学的通勤距离，而非女士到家的距离，故排除D项。"},{"no":10,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"10","options":{"A":"Went through much trouble.","B":"Commuted to save money.","C":"Studied on the bus.","D":"Lived on campus."},"script":"W: [8] I've been thinking about moving off campus next semester.\nM: Hmm... I don't know if that's a good idea as your mother and I would worry about you living all by yourself in an\napartment so far from home. [9] And then there's the expense, too. I'm not sure we can afford it.\nW: Well, I wouldn't be alone, actually, Three of my friends from the dormitory would share the place with me. And\nwith four of us, it wouldn't cost any more than living in the dorm.\nM: Are you sure about that? [9] Rents in big cities are high, and then there's the cost of electricity and gas. Have you\nfactored those in?\nW: We've budgeted for those, and for Internet and transportation. And if we move far enough from the city center, the\nrent is low enough for us to afford all of it.\nM: What's about the commute to and from campus? It sounds like you'll be wasting several hours every day on the\nroad instead of studying.\nW: [ioj It's about an hour to campus, but there's no reason I can't study on the bus. After all, I did that during high\nschool and my commute was even longer then.\nM: That's true, But I'm not sure why you want to leave campus and go through all that trouble when the cost is the\nsame.\nW: For a lot of reasons. We'd have a kitchen, so we could cook ourselves. And we'd each have our own bedroom,\nwhere we could study instead of going to the library. We just have more space and privacy.\nM: [li] I'm not convinced that the benefits are greater than the disadvantages, but I'll discuss this with your mother.","audioStart":464.7,"groupNote":"Questions 8 to 11","answer":"C","expl":"女士说她高中时做过什么？\nA）经历了很多麻烦。\nB）通勤以节省费用。\nC）在公交车上学习。\nD）住在校内。 对话中，当男士提出担心女土每天会浪费好几个小时在通勤路上而无法学习时，女士回应说她到学校大约 需要一个小时，但她完全可以在公交车上学习，毕竞高中时她就这样做过，所以答案为C项。其他选项女 土在描述自己的高中生活时均未提及，故排除。"},{"no":11,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"11","options":{"A":"Discuss the matter with the woman’s mother.","B":"Make a decision within a few days.","C":"Try his best to persuade his wife.","D":"Weigh the benefits against the disadvantages."},"script":"W: [8] I've been thinking about moving off campus next semester.\nM: Hmm... I don't know if that's a good idea as your mother and I would worry about you living all by yourself in an\napartment so far from home. [9] And then there's the expense, too. I'm not sure we can afford it.\nW: Well, I wouldn't be alone, actually, Three of my friends from the dormitory would share the place with me. And\nwith four of us, it wouldn't cost any more than living in the dorm.\nM: Are you sure about that? [9] Rents in big cities are high, and then there's the cost of electricity and gas. Have you\nfactored those in?\nW: We've budgeted for those, and for Internet and transportation. And if we move far enough from the city center, the\nrent is low enough for us to afford all of it.\nM: What's about the commute to and from campus? It sounds like you'll be wasting several hours every day on the\nroad instead of studying.\nW: [ioj It's about an hour to campus, but there's no reason I can't study on the bus. After all, I did that during high\nschool and my commute was even longer then.\nM: That's true, But I'm not sure why you want to leave campus and go through all that trouble when the cost is the\nsame.\nW: For a lot of reasons. We'd have a kitchen, so we could cook ourselves. And we'd each have our own bedroom,\nwhere we could study instead of going to the library. We just have more space and privacy.\nM: [li] I'm not convinced that the benefits are greater than the disadvantages, but I'll discuss this with your mother.","audioStart":464.7,"groupNote":"Questions 8 to 11","answer":"A","expl":"在对话结尾，男士说他将做什么？\nA）与女士的妈妈讨论此事。 C尽力说服他的妻子。\nD）权衡利弊。 B在儿天内做出决定。 解在对话结尾，男士提到自己不觉得（女士住在校外）利大于弊，但他会和女士的妈妈商量一下这件事，所 以答案为A项，由此也可以排除D项。B、C两项对话中都没有提到，故排除。 Conversation Two"},{"no":12,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"12","options":{"A":"Interviewing the man.","B":"Looking for Lawrence.","C":"Choosing a present for her nephew.","D":"Helping the man with his shopping."},"script":"W: Good morning.[12] I'm looking for a present for my nephew, Lawrence.\nM: Hi.Do you have anything in mind?\nW: I'm not sure. No. Do you know what boys aged around ten typically like?\nM: [13] For that age group, our biggest seller this year has been this action figure. It's called Bobo Man, and it's\nincredibly popular.\nW: Does it do anything?\nM: Not by itself. It has no batteries, if that's what you mean. There's a Bobo Man cartoon popular amongst young\nchildren, you see, And this toy is the lead character from the show.\nW: Oh, I understand now. I've never heard of it, but I do know little Lawrence loves cartoons. Do you have any other\npopular toys? Perhaps something educational.\nM: Yes, of course. Right over here, we have dozens of puzzles for children of all ages. They are fun, great for\nexercising the mind and can be played either alone or in groups.\nW: I like this sort of idea. [14] Lawrence has a sister who is slightly older, so maybe a game that they can play together\nwould be good\nM: What do you think of Battleships, then? [15] It's a total classic. It has been around since the 1930s\nW:Oh, yeah.[15] We had one of those when growing up.\nM: [1s] Of course you did. Everyone did. And kids enjoy it just as much today as you and I did when we were young.\nSome games just never get old.\nW: I suppose you are right. I have fond memories playing Battleships when it was raining outside and we had to stay\nindoors. That's decided then, How much?\nM:19 pounds and 90 pence,please\nW: There you are. Have a nice day.\nM: Thank you. You too.","audioStart":647.7,"groupNote":"Questions 12 to 15","answer":"C","expl":"Whatis thewoman doing?女士在做什么？\nA）采访男士。\nB）寻找劳伦斯。\nC）为侄子挑选礼物。\nD）帮助男士购物。 解+对话开头女士明确表示她要给子劳伦斯挑礼物，然后请男士推荐玩具，并最终选定了一款，所以答案为 C项。其他三项均与对话内容不符，可直接排除。"},{"no":13,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"13","options":{"A":"Quite educational.","B":"Incredibly popular.","C":"Driven by batteries.","D":"Obviously childish."},"script":"W: Good morning.[12] I'm looking for a present for my nephew, Lawrence.\nM: Hi.Do you have anything in mind?\nW: I'm not sure. No. Do you know what boys aged around ten typically like?\nM: [13] For that age group, our biggest seller this year has been this action figure. It's called Bobo Man, and it's\nincredibly popular.\nW: Does it do anything?\nM: Not by itself. It has no batteries, if that's what you mean. There's a Bobo Man cartoon popular amongst young\nchildren, you see, And this toy is the lead character from the show.\nW: Oh, I understand now. I've never heard of it, but I do know little Lawrence loves cartoons. Do you have any other\npopular toys? Perhaps something educational.\nM: Yes, of course. Right over here, we have dozens of puzzles for children of all ages. They are fun, great for\nexercising the mind and can be played either alone or in groups.\nW: I like this sort of idea. [14] Lawrence has a sister who is slightly older, so maybe a game that they can play together\nwould be good\nM: What do you think of Battleships, then? [15] It's a total classic. It has been around since the 1930s\nW:Oh, yeah.[15] We had one of those when growing up.\nM: [1s] Of course you did. Everyone did. And kids enjoy it just as much today as you and I did when we were young.\nSome games just never get old.\nW: I suppose you are right. I have fond memories playing Battleships when it was raining outside and we had to stay\nindoors. That's decided then, How much?\nM:19 pounds and 90 pence,please\nW: There you are. Have a nice day.\nM: Thank you. You too.","audioStart":647.7,"groupNote":"Questions 12 to 15","answer":"B","expl":"男士如何描迷动作玩偶波波侠？\nA）很有教育意义。\nB）特别受欢迎。\nC）由电池驱动。\nD）明显孩子气。 解+对话中，男士提到，对于十岁左右的男孩来说，今年最畅销的是一款叫做波波侠的动作玩偶，特别受欢 迎，所以答案是B项。A项“很有教育意义”是女士对她要挑选的玩具的要求，故排除：对话中男士提到 波波侠没有电池，C项与此相，故排除：D项在对话中没有相关内容，故也排除。"},{"no":14,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"14","options":{"A":"One he loves for helping exercise his mind.","B":"One he can play together with his sister.","C":"One he finds interesting and challenging.","D":"One he can play either alone or in a group."},"script":"W: Good morning.[12] I'm looking for a present for my nephew, Lawrence.\nM: Hi.Do you have anything in mind?\nW: I'm not sure. No. Do you know what boys aged around ten typically like?\nM: [13] For that age group, our biggest seller this year has been this action figure. It's called Bobo Man, and it's\nincredibly popular.\nW: Does it do anything?\nM: Not by itself. It has no batteries, if that's what you mean. There's a Bobo Man cartoon popular amongst young\nchildren, you see, And this toy is the lead character from the show.\nW: Oh, I understand now. I've never heard of it, but I do know little Lawrence loves cartoons. Do you have any other\npopular toys? Perhaps something educational.\nM: Yes, of course. Right over here, we have dozens of puzzles for children of all ages. They are fun, great for\nexercising the mind and can be played either alone or in groups.\nW: I like this sort of idea. [14] Lawrence has a sister who is slightly older, so maybe a game that they can play together\nwould be good\nM: What do you think of Battleships, then? [15] It's a total classic. It has been around since the 1930s\nW:Oh, yeah.[15] We had one of those when growing up.\nM: [1s] Of course you did. Everyone did. And kids enjoy it just as much today as you and I did when we were young.\nSome games just never get old.\nW: I suppose you are right. I have fond memories playing Battleships when it was raining outside and we had to stay\nindoors. That's decided then, How much?\nM:19 pounds and 90 pence,please\nW: There you are. Have a nice day.\nM: Thank you. You too.","audioStart":647.7,"groupNote":"Questions 12 to 15","answer":"B","expl":"女士认为什么样的游戏适合劳伦斯？\nA）一种他喜爱的且有助于锻炼思维的游戏。\nB）一种他可以和姐姐一起玩的游戏。\nC）一种他觉得有趣且具有挑战性的游戏。\nD）一种他可以独自玩或与他人一起玩的游戏。 解+ 对话中，女士提到劳伦斯有个姐姐，年纪比他稍大一点，所以也许一款他们可以一起玩的游戏会很好，因 对拼图游戏的描述，并不是女士的需求，故均排除：C项在对话中没有相关内容。故也排除。"},{"no":15,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"15","options":{"A":"It has been enjoyed by kids for decades.","B":"It always reminds people of times of war.","C":"It has been played both indoors and outdoors.","D":"It distinguishes itself as one of the oldest games."},"script":"W: Good morning.[12] I'm looking for a present for my nephew, Lawrence.\nM: Hi.Do you have anything in mind?\nW: I'm not sure. No. Do you know what boys aged around ten typically like?\nM: [13] For that age group, our biggest seller this year has been this action figure. It's called Bobo Man, and it's\nincredibly popular.\nW: Does it do anything?\nM: Not by itself. It has no batteries, if that's what you mean. There's a Bobo Man cartoon popular amongst young\nchildren, you see, And this toy is the lead character from the show.\nW: Oh, I understand now. I've never heard of it, but I do know little Lawrence loves cartoons. Do you have any other\npopular toys? Perhaps something educational.\nM: Yes, of course. Right over here, we have dozens of puzzles for children of all ages. They are fun, great for\nexercising the mind and can be played either alone or in groups.\nW: I like this sort of idea. [14] Lawrence has a sister who is slightly older, so maybe a game that they can play together\nwould be good\nM: What do you think of Battleships, then? [15] It's a total classic. It has been around since the 1930s\nW:Oh, yeah.[15] We had one of those when growing up.\nM: [1s] Of course you did. Everyone did. And kids enjoy it just as much today as you and I did when we were young.\nSome games just never get old.\nW: I suppose you are right. I have fond memories playing Battleships when it was raining outside and we had to stay\nindoors. That's decided then, How much?\nM:19 pounds and 90 pence,please\nW: There you are. Have a nice day.\nM: Thank you. You too.","audioStart":647.7,"groupNote":"Questions 12 to 15","answer":"A","expl":"关于海战棋游戏，我们从对话中了解到什么？\nA）它几十年来一直受孩子们的喜爱。\nB）它总是让人想起战争时期\nC）它在室内和室外都可以玩。\nD）它作为最古老的游戏之一脱颖而出 对话中，男士向女士推荐海战棋游戏时提到它是一款经典游戏，从上世纪30年代就有了，女士回应说他们 小时候就有一款，男士接着说每个人都有过这款游戏，而且现在的孩子和他们小时候一样喜欢它。由此可 知，海战棋游戏儿十年来一直受孩子们的喜爱，答案是A项。B项在对话中没有相关信息，故排除：对话 中虽提到，女士说自已有过在外面下雨不得不待在室内时玩海战棋的美好回忆，但并未提及海战棋游戏在 室内和室外都可以玩，故排除C项：虽然对话中提到海战棋游戏从上世纪30年代就有了，但是并没有明确 说明它是最古老的游戏之一，故排除D项。 Section Passage One"},{"no":16,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"16","options":{"A":"A display of a variety of products increases consumers’ interest.","B":"Too many choices decrease one’s desire to buy.","C":"Fewer choices lead to lower customer satisfaction.","D":"Customers given more choices are more likely to get confused."},"script":"We assume that if we offer our customers more choices, then they'll be more likely to buy our products. [l6] But\nhere's the paradox of choice: if a person is presented with too many choices, they are actually less likely to buy. [17] In\n2012,psychologists published a study about jams.On a regular day at a local food market,people would find a display\ntable with 24 different kinds of jams. Then on another day, at that same food market, people were given only 6 different\ntypes of jam choices. Guess which display table led to more sales?\nThe study found that while the bigger display table created more interest, people were almost 10 times less likely\nto purchase a jar of jam than in the case of the smaller display. Customer satisfaction takes a hit as well. In the study.\nthe bigger display of jams leads to lower customer satisfaction than the smaller display. In an extensive study published\nin 2015, researchers analyzed a total of 99 choice studies and specifically looked at those cases in which reducing\nchoices helped to boost sales. [is] They found four rules that motivate consumers to buy: when people want to make a\nquick and easy choice, when the product is complex, when it's difficult to compare alternatives, and when consumers\ndon't have clear preferences, So as you can see, when it comes to choices, less is more.","audioStart":872.5,"groupNote":"Questions 16 to 18","answer":"B","expl":"What do we learn from the passage about the paradox of choice? 关于“选择悸论”，我们从文章中了解到什么？\nB）选择太多会降低人们的购买欲望。 表明，当人们面对过多的选择时，他们购买的可能性反而更低。选项B与文章表述一致，故为正确答案。 虽然文章中提到大展示台吸引了更多关注，但这并非“选择存论”涉及的方面，故排除A选项。文章表 明更少的果酱种类展示带来的顾客满意度更高，C选项与原文相悸，故排除。文章中未提及顾客是否会困 惑，属于无中生有，故D选项可排除。\nD）被给予更多选择的顾客更有可能感到困惑。"},{"no":17,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"17","options":{"A":"What kind of display results in more sales.","B":"What kind of display creates more interest.","C":"What kind of display attracts a greater crowd of customers.","D":"What kind of display leads to greater customer satisfaction."},"script":"We assume that if we offer our customers more choices, then they'll be more likely to buy our products. [l6] But\nhere's the paradox of choice: if a person is presented with too many choices, they are actually less likely to buy. [17] In\n2012,psychologists published a study about jams.On a regular day at a local food market,people would find a display\ntable with 24 different kinds of jams. Then on another day, at that same food market, people were given only 6 different\ntypes of jam choices. Guess which display table led to more sales?\nThe study found that while the bigger display table created more interest, people were almost 10 times less likely\nto purchase a jar of jam than in the case of the smaller display. Customer satisfaction takes a hit as well. In the study.\nthe bigger display of jams leads to lower customer satisfaction than the smaller display. In an extensive study published\nin 2015, researchers analyzed a total of 99 choice studies and specifically looked at those cases in which reducing\nchoices helped to boost sales. [is] They found four rules that motivate consumers to buy: when people want to make a\nquick and easy choice, when the product is complex, when it's difficult to compare alternatives, and when consumers\ndon't have clear preferences, So as you can see, when it comes to choices, less is more.","audioStart":872.5,"groupNote":"Questions 16 to 18","answer":"A","expl":"在2012年关于果音的研究中，心理学家想要探究什么？\nA）存在顾客什么都不买的情况。\nB）存在提高顾客满意度的技巧。 文章最后提到，2015年发表的大规模研究发现了四条促使消费者购买的规则，D选项与原文表述相符，故 为正确答案。文中未提及顾客什么都不买的情况，A选项无中生有，故排除。该研究发现的是促使消费者 购买的规则，并非提高顾客满意度的技巧和预测顾客偏好，B和C选项可排除。 Passage Two\nC）存在预测顾客偏好的方法。\nD）存在激励顾客购买的规则。"},{"no":18,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"18","options":{"A":"There are situations where customers buy nothing.","B":"There are tricks to increase customer satisfaction.","C":"There are ways to predict customer preferences.","D":"There are rules that motivate customers to buy."},"script":"We assume that if we offer our customers more choices, then they'll be more likely to buy our products. [l6] But\nhere's the paradox of choice: if a person is presented with too many choices, they are actually less likely to buy. [17] In\n2012,psychologists published a study about jams.On a regular day at a local food market,people would find a display\ntable with 24 different kinds of jams. Then on another day, at that same food market, people were given only 6 different\ntypes of jam choices. Guess which display table led to more sales?\nThe study found that while the bigger display table created more interest, people were almost 10 times less likely\nto purchase a jar of jam than in the case of the smaller display. Customer satisfaction takes a hit as well. In the study.\nthe bigger display of jams leads to lower customer satisfaction than the smaller display. In an extensive study published\nin 2015, researchers analyzed a total of 99 choice studies and specifically looked at those cases in which reducing\nchoices helped to boost sales. [is] They found four rules that motivate consumers to buy: when people want to make a\nquick and easy choice, when the product is complex, when it's difficult to compare alternatives, and when consumers\ndon't have clear preferences, So as you can see, when it comes to choices, less is more.","audioStart":872.5,"groupNote":"Questions 16 to 18","answer":"D","expl":"【答案】D（细节题）There are rules that motivate customers to buy. 【定位】P2They found four rules that motivate consumers to buy: when people want to make a quick and easy choice, when the product is complex, when it's difficult to compare alternatives, and when consumers don't have clear preferences.他们发现了促使消费者购买的四条规则：当人们想做一个又快又省事的选择时、当产品很复杂时、当难以比较各个备选项时、以及当消费者没有明确偏好时。 【信号】found 加冒号列举，结论句标志。原文 They found four rules that motivate consumers to buy以 found 引出、冒号后接四条列举，是这项 2015 研究的总结论句，正是“finding”所在。 【替换】four rules that motivate consumers to buy↔There are rules that motivate customers to buy.“motivate consumers to buy”=“motivate customers to buy”，近乎同形 【排除】\nA）存在顾客什么都不买的情况。\nB）存在提高顾客满意度的技巧。\nC）存在预测顾客偏好的方法。\nD）存在激励顾客购买的规律。"},{"no":19,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"19","options":{"A":"Stressful.","B":"Burdensome.","C":"Worthwhile.","D":"Energy-boosting."},"script":"The New Year has come and gone, and many may have already forgotten what they had resolved to do on January\n1st. But if that resolution was to volunteer more, now is the best time to start. Volunteering can drop after the holiday\nseason, and by February, some organizations lack the volunteers they had in surplus just a month ago. [19] According to\none survey,only 26% of Americans volunteered, despite reports that many Americans view volunteering as worthwhile.\nWhy such a disconnect? [2o] Most people cite time and conflicting work schedules as major barriers, but\nemployees and employers would actually benefit if companies made employee volunteering part of the job. Researchers\nhave found donating our time and talent to others can lower blood pressure, boost happiness and improve mental and physical health. Studies have also linked employee volunteer programs with more productive employees. With so much emphasis on employee wellness these days, company volunteer programs could be the perfect 2025 resolution. But\nfor volunteering to benefit employees and the workplace, the entire organization needs to be involved. By allowing\nemployees to donate their time during work hours, the workplace becomes an outlet for personal fulfillment without\naffecting personal time.[21] In one survey. 89% of employees perceived companies that sponsored volunteer activities\nas creating a better working environment, and 75% said volunteering was critical to their well-being.","audioStart":1042.3,"groupNote":"Questions 19 to 21","answer":"C","expl":"【答案】C（态度观点题）Worthwhile. 【定位】P1According to one survey, only 26% of Americans volunteered, despite reports that many Americans view volunteering as worthwhile.据一项调查，只有 26% 的美国人做过志愿服务，尽管有报道称许多美国人认为志愿服务是值得做的。 【信号】题干同词 many Americans 在 despite 让步从句里现身。答案句里 many Americans view volunteering as worthwhile 与题干 many Americans 完全对上，且 despite（尽管）这个让步信号后面跟的正是‘大家的看法’，听到 despite + many Americans 就该锁定这句。 【替换】view volunteering as worthwhile↔Worthwhile.原句形容词被直接抽出做选项 【排除】\nA）有压力的。\nB）繁重的。\nC）值得的。\nD）提升精力的。"},{"no":20,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"20","options":{"A":"By linking it with employees’ salary.","B":"By taking it as the New Year resolution.","C":"By improving work schedules.","D":"By making it part of the job."},"script":"The New Year has come and gone, and many may have already forgotten what they had resolved to do on January\n1st. But if that resolution was to volunteer more, now is the best time to start. Volunteering can drop after the holiday\nseason, and by February, some organizations lack the volunteers they had in surplus just a month ago. [19] According to\none survey,only 26% of Americans volunteered, despite reports that many Americans view volunteering as worthwhile.\nWhy such a disconnect? [2o] Most people cite time and conflicting work schedules as major barriers, but\nemployees and employers would actually benefit if companies made employee volunteering part of the job. Researchers\nhave found donating our time and talent to others can lower blood pressure, boost happiness and improve mental and physical health. Studies have also linked employee volunteer programs with more productive employees. With so much emphasis on employee wellness these days, company volunteer programs could be the perfect 2025 resolution. But\nfor volunteering to benefit employees and the workplace, the entire organization needs to be involved. By allowing\nemployees to donate their time during work hours, the workplace becomes an outlet for personal fulfillment without\naffecting personal time.[21] In one survey. 89% of employees perceived companies that sponsored volunteer activities\nas creating a better working environment, and 75% said volunteering was critical to their well-being.","audioStart":1042.3,"groupNote":"Questions 19 to 21","answer":"D","expl":"公司怎样才能确保员工和履主都从志愿服务中受益？\nA）通过将具与员工工资挂钩。 C通过改进工作安排。\nB）通过将其作为新年决心。\nD）通过使其成为工作的一部分。 解文章中间部分提到，如果公司将员工志愿服务作为工作的一部分，员工和雇主都将从中受益，D选项符合 文意，故为正确答案。文章未提及将志愿服务和工资关联的内容，故排除A选项。新年决心只是文章开头 引人话题的内容，并非公司让双方受益的方式，故排除B选项。文章强调的是公司把志愿服务作为员工工 作的一部分，而非改进工作安排，C选项与原文不符，故排除。"},{"no":21,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"21","options":{"A":"It was very important to their well-being.","B":"It had taken little of their spare time.","C":"It was an outlet for their personal fulfillment.","D":"It helped improve their working environment."},"script":"The New Year has come and gone, and many may have already forgotten what they had resolved to do on January\n1st. But if that resolution was to volunteer more, now is the best time to start. Volunteering can drop after the holiday\nseason, and by February, some organizations lack the volunteers they had in surplus just a month ago. [19] According to\none survey,only 26% of Americans volunteered, despite reports that many Americans view volunteering as worthwhile.\nWhy such a disconnect? [2o] Most people cite time and conflicting work schedules as major barriers, but\nemployees and employers would actually benefit if companies made employee volunteering part of the job. Researchers\nhave found donating our time and talent to others can lower blood pressure, boost happiness and improve mental and physical health. Studies have also linked employee volunteer programs with more productive employees. With so much emphasis on employee wellness these days, company volunteer programs could be the perfect 2025 resolution. But\nfor volunteering to benefit employees and the workplace, the entire organization needs to be involved. By allowing\nemployees to donate their time during work hours, the workplace becomes an outlet for personal fulfillment without\naffecting personal time.[21] In one survey. 89% of employees perceived companies that sponsored volunteer activities\nas creating a better working environment, and 75% said volunteering was critical to their well-being.","audioStart":1042.3,"groupNote":"Questions 19 to 21","answer":"A","expl":"在一项调查中，75%的员工对志愿服务有何评价？\nA）这对他们的健康快乐非常重要。 D这有助于改善他们的工作环境\nB）这没占用他们多少业余时间 文章最后提到，在一项调查中，89%的员工认为宽助志愿活动的公司能营造更好的工作环境，75%的员工 表示志愿服务对他们的健康快乐至关重要。A选项中的veryimportant是原文中的critical的同义转述，故为 正确答案。文中未提及志愿服务没占用员工多少业余时间的内容，属于无中生有，故排除B选项。文中 提到职场可成为实现个人价值的渠道，但并未指出这是这项调查中75%的员工对志愿服务的看法，故可排 除C选项。原文表明89%的员工认为费助志愿活动的公司能营造更好的工作环境，并非75%员工对志愿服 务的评价，故排除D选项 Passage Three\nC）这是他们实现个人价值的一个集道，"},{"no":22,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"22","options":{"A":"It amounts to 2,000 tons daily.","B":"It mostly comes from supermarkets.","C":"It is estimated at 11% of the UK’s total food supply.","D":"It has a lot to do with independent luxury food suppliers."},"script":"[22] The vast majority of the UK's 200,000 tonnes of retail sectors annual food waste comes from supermarkets.\nBut it's a problem that impacts independent luxury food suppliers too. The scale is small. It's estimated at less than one\nper cent of the UK's food waste total. But the quality from these suppliers is high. Everything from specialist cheeses to\nsought-after coffee beans gets thrown away every day in the current food system.\nThis is where French entrepreneur Sophie André comes in. [23] In 2016 she moved to London and began\nby setting up a business delivering luxury hand-made food across the city.\"Food delivery seems to be a big thing\nhere,\" She says,\"So I started delivering products by bicycle. Talking with local producers I learned that a small part of the production is sometimes wasted due to problems with size or shape, but the quality of the products remains unchanged.\"[24] Unlike many food waste projects that have sprung up in recent years, the idea is not simply to\nredistribute waste food, but to provide a high-quality product. André's company Elysia offers breakfasts and snacks specifically aimed at business meetings and events. Once sourced, Elysia uses waste products to create \"simple, natural, straightforward\"meals and snacks.[25] Perhaps unlike other waste food, the supply is regular. Suppliers will always\nhave a certain amount of surplus. So the menus remain regular too. André believes this benefits both her business and\nher customers.","audioStart":1223.2,"groupNote":"Questions 22 to 25","answer":"B","expl":"关于英国零售业的食物浪费，我们了解到什么？\nA）它每天达到2000吨\nB）它主要来自超市。\nC）据估计，它占英国食品总供应量的11%。\nD）它与独立的高端食品供应商有很大关系 解→文章开头提到，英国零售业每年产生的20万吨食物浪费中，绝大部分来自超市。B选项符合原文，故为正 确答案。根据文章内容，每年产生20万吨食物浪费，通过换算可知每天浪费的数量为数百吨，而非2000 吨，故排除A选项。文中提到独立高踏食品供应商的浪费量不足英国食物浪费总量的1%，并未提及11%这 一数据，故排除C、D两个选项。"},{"no":23,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"23","options":{"A":"It rents bicycles to deliver food.","B":"It sells sought-after coffee beans.","C":"It delivers luxury hand-made food.","D":"It recycles waste food from the city."},"script":"[22] The vast majority of the UK's 200,000 tonnes of retail sectors annual food waste comes from supermarkets.\nBut it's a problem that impacts independent luxury food suppliers too. The scale is small. It's estimated at less than one\nper cent of the UK's food waste total. But the quality from these suppliers is high. Everything from specialist cheeses to\nsought-after coffee beans gets thrown away every day in the current food system.\nThis is where French entrepreneur Sophie André comes in. [23] In 2016 she moved to London and began\nby setting up a business delivering luxury hand-made food across the city.\"Food delivery seems to be a big thing\nhere,\" She says,\"So I started delivering products by bicycle. Talking with local producers I learned that a small part of the production is sometimes wasted due to problems with size or shape, but the quality of the products remains unchanged.\"[24] Unlike many food waste projects that have sprung up in recent years, the idea is not simply to\nredistribute waste food, but to provide a high-quality product. André's company Elysia offers breakfasts and snacks specifically aimed at business meetings and events. Once sourced, Elysia uses waste products to create \"simple, natural, straightforward\"meals and snacks.[25] Perhaps unlike other waste food, the supply is regular. Suppliers will always\nhave a certain amount of surplus. So the menus remain regular too. André believes this benefits both her business and\nher customers.","audioStart":1223.2,"groupNote":"Questions 22 to 25","answer":"C","expl":"索菲安德熟在伦敦的生意是做什么的？\nA）它出租自行车来配送食物。\nC）它配送高端手工制作食品。 B它销售备受追捧的咖啡豆\nD）它回收来自城市的废弃食品 解+文章中间提到，2016年，她（索非·安德烈）搬到伦敦，创办了一家在全城配送高端手工制作食品的公 司，可知索菲·安德烈在伦敦创办的公司是配送高端手工制作食品的，C选项与原文一致，故为正确答 案。文中提到索非·安德烈最初是用自行车配送食品，而非出租自行车用于配送，A选项曲解文意。文中 提到每天包括备受追捧的咖啡豆在内的食品被丢弃，并未提及索菲·安德烈是否销售咖啡豆，故排除B选 项。公司虽利用废弃食品制作产品，但核心是配送高端手工制作食品，回收城市废弃食品并非其主要业 务，故D选项错误。"},{"no":24,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"24","options":{"A":"It pays special attention to the size and shape of its products.","B":"It tries to escape the constraints of the current food system.","C":"It cooperates closely with local producers.","D":"It aims to provide high-quality products."},"script":"[22] The vast majority of the UK's 200,000 tonnes of retail sectors annual food waste comes from supermarkets.\nBut it's a problem that impacts independent luxury food suppliers too. The scale is small. It's estimated at less than one\nper cent of the UK's food waste total. But the quality from these suppliers is high. Everything from specialist cheeses to\nsought-after coffee beans gets thrown away every day in the current food system.\nThis is where French entrepreneur Sophie André comes in. [23] In 2016 she moved to London and began\nby setting up a business delivering luxury hand-made food across the city.\"Food delivery seems to be a big thing\nhere,\" She says,\"So I started delivering products by bicycle. Talking with local producers I learned that a small part of the production is sometimes wasted due to problems with size or shape, but the quality of the products remains unchanged.\"[24] Unlike many food waste projects that have sprung up in recent years, the idea is not simply to\nredistribute waste food, but to provide a high-quality product. André's company Elysia offers breakfasts and snacks specifically aimed at business meetings and events. Once sourced, Elysia uses waste products to create \"simple, natural, straightforward\"meals and snacks.[25] Perhaps unlike other waste food, the supply is regular. Suppliers will always\nhave a certain amount of surplus. So the menus remain regular too. André believes this benefits both her business and\nher customers.","audioStart":1223.2,"groupNote":"Questions 22 to 25","answer":"D","expl":"In what way does Sophie Andre's business differ from many food waste projects? 索菲·安德烈的生意与许多食物浪费处理项目有何不同？\nA）它特别关注具产品的尺寸和形状\nB）它试图摆脱当前食品体系的限制\nC）它与当地生产商密切合作。\nD）它旨在提供高质量的产品 解+文章中提到，与近年来通现出的许多食物浪费处理项目不同，安德烈的想法不只是重新分配废弃食品，而 是要提供高品质的产品。也就是说，安德烈的生意与其他食物浪费处理项自的不同之处在于她的生意旨 在提供高品质产品，D选项正确。文中提及伦敦本地食品生产商将部分因尺寸或形状不符合要求的食品废 弃，并未提及安德烈的公司特别关注产品尺寸和形状，故A选项错误。文中未提及安德烈的公司试图摆脱 当前食品体系的限制，故排除B选项。与当地生产商合作是获取废弃食品的方式，而并非是安德烈的公司 与其他食物浪费处理项目的区别所在，C选项错误。"},{"no":25,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"25","options":{"A":"To ensure a regular supply.","B":"To maintain a unique menu.","C":"To make up for the waste products.","D":"To meet customers’ urgent demand."},"script":"[22] The vast majority of the UK's 200,000 tonnes of retail sectors annual food waste comes from supermarkets.\nBut it's a problem that impacts independent luxury food suppliers too. The scale is small. It's estimated at less than one\nper cent of the UK's food waste total. But the quality from these suppliers is high. Everything from specialist cheeses to\nsought-after coffee beans gets thrown away every day in the current food system.\nThis is where French entrepreneur Sophie André comes in. [23] In 2016 she moved to London and began\nby setting up a business delivering luxury hand-made food across the city.\"Food delivery seems to be a big thing\nhere,\" She says,\"So I started delivering products by bicycle. Talking with local producers I learned that a small part of the production is sometimes wasted due to problems with size or shape, but the quality of the products remains unchanged.\"[24] Unlike many food waste projects that have sprung up in recent years, the idea is not simply to\nredistribute waste food, but to provide a high-quality product. André's company Elysia offers breakfasts and snacks specifically aimed at business meetings and events. Once sourced, Elysia uses waste products to create \"simple, natural, straightforward\"meals and snacks.[25] Perhaps unlike other waste food, the supply is regular. Suppliers will always\nhave a certain amount of surplus. So the menus remain regular too. André believes this benefits both her business and\nher customers.","audioStart":1223.2,"groupNote":"Questions 22 to 25","answer":"A","expl":"为什么供应商总会有一定数量的盈余产品？\nA）为了确保稳定的供应。 C为了弥补废弃产品\nB）为了维持独特的菜单\nD）为了满足客户的紧急需求 解文章最后提到，或许与其他废弃食品不同，这些食品的供应很稳定，供应商总会有一定数量的盈余食 品，所以菜单也能保持稳定。由此可知，供应商总会有一定数量的盈余产品，使得公司的菜单能够保持 稳定，从而确保产品供应的稳定性，满足业务需求，A选项符合文意，故为答案。文章强调的是菜单保持 “regular（稳定的）”，而非“unique（独特的）”，且保持菜单稳定是确保稳定供应带来的结果，并非 供应商有盈余产品的原因，B选项错误。虽然生产过程中存在因尺寸或形状问题导致的浪费但原文未提 及供应商储备盈余产品是为了弥补这些浪费，C选项属于过度推断。文中未提及客户存在紧急需求，也未 表明供应商的盈余产品与满足紧急需求相关，D选项属于无中生有。 28四#20256第 Part 11 ReadingComprehension SectionA 本文节选自2024年5月17日发表于TheFamousPeople上的一篇标题为“AlfredHitchcockBiography 的文章。文章主要介绍了英国电影导演阿尔弗雷德·希区柯克。他被誉为“悬念大师”，是电影史上最 具影响力的导演之一。其作品以扣人心弦的悬疑惊惊为特色，主角多为陷入困境的普通人，剧情充满欺 诈、谋杀等元素与反转，众多杰作历经时间考验，至今仍启发着电影制作人并为全球观众所喜爱。"},{"no":26,"part":"reading","kind":"cloze","stem":"第 26 空","options":{},"passage":"cloze","answerText":"particularly","answer":"G","expl":"特别，尤其） 空格所在句子主系表结构完整，故空格处应填副词，修饰后面的形容词apparent。 语法判 语义判断前一句提到“大脑拥有一种近乎神奇的改变能力”，本句意为“这一特性在一项新研究中 明显”。代人选项可知，particularly符合此处语境。Particularlyapparent（尤为明显）既呼应前文中almost magical的强调语气，又通过focusingon...hemispherectomy的限定场景突出了大脑这一能力的强大。"},{"no":27,"part":"reading","kind":"cloze","stem":"第 27 空","options":{},"passage":"cloze","answerText":"organ","answer":"F","expl":"F)organ（n.器官） [语法判断 空格位于形容词critical后，故空格处应填名词。 的一半， 义判断前一句提及hemispherectomy（大脑半球切除术），本句指出“尽管缺失这一关键 参与者仍能正常生活”。代人选项可知，organ符合此处语境。critical organ与hemispherectomy形成手术对 象的呼应。"},{"no":28,"part":"reading","kind":"cloze","stem":"第 28 空","options":{},"passage":"cloze","answerText":"remaining","answer":"H","expl":"H）remaining（adj.剩余的，剩下的） 空格前面为定冠词the，后面为名词短语halfof，故空格处应填形容词，修饰后面的名词。 语法判新 语义判新】 half与missinghalf形成对比，空格所填词与missing（缺失的）语义 脑中 一半得到了强化”。 相对，故答案为remaining。"},{"no":29,"part":"reading","kind":"cloze","stem":"第 29 空","options":{},"passage":"cloze","answerText":"indicates","answer":"C","expl":"C）indicates（表明，显示） 语法判断空格前面为句子主语This，后面为that引导的宾语从句，空格处应填请语动词。前文时态为一般现 在时，故空格处应填动词的第三人称单数形式。 语义判断前一句提到研究人员发现，接受过大脑半球切除术的人，其大脑不同网络各部分之间的连接 （及由此产生的信息交流），实际上比大脑完整的人更强”，本句结论为“大脑能够弥补某些脑结构的损 失”a前后句是研究发现与结论之间的推导关系，故此处应填indicates"},{"no":30,"part":"reading","kind":"cloze","stem":"第 30 空","options":{},"passage":"cloze","answerText":"literature","answer":"E","expl":"文献，资料） 语法判断空格前面为形容词medical，后面为that引导的定语从句，故空格处应填名词。 存在许多其他案例”代人选项 语义判前文提到了大脑半球切除术的案例，本句指出“医学 可知，medicalliterature（医学文献）符合此处语境，故答案为literature"},{"no":31,"part":"reading","kind":"cloze","stem":"第 31 空","options":{},"passage":"cloze","answerText":"adapt","answer":"A","expl":"A)adapt（适应） 空格位于句未，前面为abilityto，故空格处应填动词原形，构成不定式结构，充当ability的后置定语。 语法判 前文提到大脑有形成新网络和新连接的能力，从而能够弥补某些脑结构的损失，这强调了大脑具 语义判断 能力”，进一步 有神奇的适应能力。本句意为“医学文献中存在许多其他案例，记载了大脑惊人的 强调大脑的适应能力。故答案为adapl。"},{"no":32,"part":"reading","kind":"cloze","stem":"第 32 空","options":{},"passage":"cloze","answerText":"responsible","answer":"I","expl":"负责的） 语法判图空格前面为名词短语thepartofthebrain，后面为介词短语forsight，空格处应填分词或形容词，作 后置定语。 视觉的区 语义判断本句意为#一个小男孩因医疗原因切除了很大一部分大脑，其中包括大脑中 域”。responsiblefor（对负责）符合此处语境，故答案为responsible"},{"no":33,"part":"reading","kind":"cloze","stem":"第 33 空","options":{},"passage":"cloze","answerText":"still","answer":"K","expl":"K）still（adv仍然，还） 语法判厨空格前面为hecould，后面为动词原形see，故空格处应填人副词，修饰see。 前文提到“一个小男孩因医疗原因切除了很大一部分大脑，其中包括大脑中负责视觉的区域”： 能够 正常来说，视觉区域的缺失会导致该男孩核失明，但本句提到“但在手术数年后，科学家发现他 看见东西”still（仍然）符合此处语境，表明视觉能力一直没有消失，故答案为still。"},{"no":34,"part":"reading","kind":"cloze","stem":"第 34 空","options":{},"passage":"cloze","answerText":"visual","answer":"O","expl":"O）visual（adj视觉的，视力的） 语法翔断 空格前面为所有格themissingpart's，后面为名词tasks，故空格处应填人形容词或分词，修饰tasks。 语义判断 前文提到“切除了很大一部分大脑，其中包括大脑中负责视觉的区域·仍然能够看见东. 西”，本句解释原因“其大脑其他区域接管了缺失部分的 故答案为visual。 40四级2025.6第三座 任务”。视觉区域当然是负责视觉任务，"},{"no":35,"part":"reading","kind":"cloze","stem":"第 35 空","options":{},"passage":"cloze","answerText":"retained","answer":"J","expl":"【答案】J。 【解析】第35空作 these individuals 的谓语，宾语为 the ability to smell。该段说缺少嗅觉处理脑区的人仍能闻到气味，研究者尚不清楚他们如何保留这种能力；J 项 retained（保留）符合。O 项 visual 是形容词，不能作谓语，故选 J。"},{"no":36,"part":"reading","kind":"matching","stem":"36. The age-long mystery has now been resolved as to how phantom traffic jams are caused.","options":{},"passage":"matching","answer":"C","expl":"【答案】C段（强锚点） 【定位】C 段The exact cause of these phantom traffic jams has long remained a mystery. But now, new research has found the cause这种幻影堵车的确切成因长期以来一直是个谜。但如今，新研究已经找到了病因。 【锚点】谜题已解 【改写】has now been resolved↔new research has found the cause解决=找到病因"},{"no":37,"part":"reading","kind":"matching","stem":"37. It is possible to nearly double the hourly throughput per lane by adopting bilateral control.","options":{},"passage":"matching","answer":"H","expl":"【答案】H。 【解析】定位 H 段。该段先给出每车道每小时约1800辆的通行量，再指出采用双边控制几乎可以翻倍，直接对应 nearly double the hourly throughput per lane，故选 H。"},{"no":38,"part":"reading","kind":"matching","stem":"38. According to Horn, drivers making constant adjustments for equal spacing between cars in a flow of traffic can help reduce phantom traffic jams.","options":{},"passage":"matching","answer":"E","expl":"【答案】E段（弱锚点） 【定位】E 段In essence, each driver would constantly make adjustments to keep their car roughly halfway between the car in front and the car behind.本质上，每个司机都要不断调整，让自己的车大致保持在前车与后车的正中间。 【锚点】不断调整保持等距 【改写】making constant adjustments for equal spacing between cars↔constantly make adjustments to keep their car roughly halfway等距即居中 【辨邻】CC 段 maintaining equal spacing between cars on the road 只是概括性地点出解法叫“保持等距”，并没有讲“司机不断调整保持居中”这一具体动作，落点比 E 段浅。maintaining equal spacing between cars on the road"},{"no":39,"part":"reading","kind":"matching","stem":"39. Before systems for bilateral control are installed, drivers should not follow the car in front too closely so as to reduce phantom traffic jams.","options":{},"passage":"matching","answer":"N","expl":"【答案】N。 【解析】定位 N 段。原文说在相关系统成为标准配置之前，司机只要不跟前车太近，就能减少幽灵堵车；题干的系统普及前、不要紧跟前车均与此句一致，故选 N。"},{"no":40,"part":"reading","kind":"matching","stem":"40. Horn said with some simple modifications to the existing equipment in new vehicles, their spacing bilateral control could be accomplished.","options":{},"passage":"matching","answer":"F","expl":"【答案】F。 【解析】定位 F 段。Horn 说只需对许多新车已配备的自适应巡航控制进行相对简单的改装，就可实现双边控制，正对应题干 existing equipment 和 simple modifications，故选 F。"},{"no":41,"part":"reading","kind":"matching","stem":"41. Drivers caught up in phantom traffic jams are likely to think an accident has happened or some construction is going on.","options":{},"passage":"matching","answer":"B","expl":"【答案】B。 【解析】定位 B 段。被困于这种堵车的司机通常以为前方发生事故或正在施工，assume 对应 think，故选 B。D 段解释减速波如何向后传播，不是司机的猜测。"},{"no":42,"part":"reading","kind":"matching","stem":"42. Two sets of sensors would be used to help drivers determine the distances between cars.","options":{},"passage":"matching","answer":"M","expl":"【答案】M。 【解析】定位 M 段。现有系统使用前向传感器测前车距离，双边控制再加后向传感器测后车距离，两者合为题干所说两组传感器，故选 M。F 段只是概述改装，未给出这两组传感器的具体职责。"},{"no":43,"part":"reading","kind":"matching","stem":"43. Solving the problem of phantom jams could save America billions of dollars every year.","options":{},"passage":"matching","answer":"G","expl":"【答案】G。 【解析】定位 G 段。文章估计堵车每年给美国经济带来1210亿美元损失，解决幽灵堵车可节省其中至少一部分，对应 save America billions of dollars every year，故选 G。"},{"no":44,"part":"reading","kind":"matching","stem":"44. In a phantom traffic jam, the cars in the same lane slow down one after another, creating a wave-like effect.","options":{},"passage":"matching","answer":"D","expl":"【答案】D。 【解析】定位 D 段。原文说减速动作像波浪一样沿同车道向后传播，且传播越远越严重，正对应 one after another 和 wave-like effect，故选 D。"},{"no":45,"part":"reading","kind":"matching","stem":"45. Horn has found that certain creatures use strategies similar to bilateral control to avoid hitting each other while moving in dense crowds.","options":{},"passage":"matching","answer":"L","expl":"【答案】L。 【解析】定位 L 段。Horn 发现鱼群、鸟群和蝙蝠在密集移动时采用类似间距策略以避免相撞，与题干 certain creatures 和 similar strategies 对应，故选 L。"},{"no":46,"part":"reading","kind":"mcq","stem":"46. What do we learn about American society from the first paragraph?","options":{"A":"It sees lots of personal income wasted on beauty products.","B":"It considers one’s physical appearance very important.","C":"It places a high value on the physical fitness of stars.","D":"It expects every individual to look their very best."},"passage":"passage1","answer":"B","expl":"【答案】B。 【解析】第一段首句直接指出社会高度重视外貌美，随后用每年外貌开支和模仿明星的例子说明。本卷 B 项概括了这一点。花钱的事实不等于作者认定浪费，故排除 A，答案为 B。"},{"no":47,"part":"reading","kind":"mcq","stem":"47. What happens when people strive to look better?","options":{"A":"They have to get prepared for resentment and bitter court battles.","B":"They are better able to gain attention, love, and personal connection.","C":"They may neglect to cultivate the inner qualities that matter more.","D":"They feel much of their precious time, energy and resources is wasted."},"passage":"passage1","answer":"C","expl":"【答案】C。 【解析】定位第二、三段。过度追求外貌可能使人忽略能带来更深亲密关系、满足感和意义的内在品质，对应 C 项。原文说耗费时间精力金钱，但没有说人们主观上感到这些全被浪费，故不选 D。"},{"no":48,"part":"reading","kind":"mcq","stem":"48. What does the author say about outer beauty?","options":{"A":"It may be inherited or cultivated.","B":"It may lead to bad as well as good habits.","C":"It may create connection or isolation.","D":"It may do as much harm as good."},"passage":"passage1","answer":"D","expl":"【答案】D。 【解析】该句明确表示外貌美可能带来与好处同样多的坏处，D 项是同义概括。文中只提到一味维持完美外表的习惯，没有同时说会形成好习惯，因此 B 不成立，故选 D。"},{"no":49,"part":"reading","kind":"mcq","stem":"49. What brings about spiritual connection?","options":{"A":"Meeting of minds.","B":"Attraction at first sight.","C":"Physical chemistry.","D":"Frequent interaction."},"passage":"passage1","answer":"A","expl":"【答案】A。 【解析】定位讨论深层连接的句子。作者说，内心世界的相遇才创造持久的亲密和心灵联系，A 项 Meeting of minds 是同义表达。D 项频繁互动并非作者此处给出的原因，故选 A。"},{"no":50,"part":"reading","kind":"mcq","stem":"50. What happens when we take the path toward cultivating inner beauty?","options":{"A":"We may find a route toward other people’s souls.","B":"We may be appreciated more by people around us.","C":"We will meet people who value us for who we are.","D":"We will be discouraged from revealing our true selves."},"passage":"passage1","answer":"C","expl":"当我们踏上培养内在美的道路时会发生什么？ C我们会遇到珍视我们真实自我的人。\nA）我们可能找到一条通往他人灵魂的路径。\nB）我们可能获得身边人更多的欣赏 四级2025.6鄂三45 根据题千关键词cultivatinginnerbeauty定位到最后一段末句。 定位 最后一段未句提到，终有一天，我们会遇见那些与我们灵魂相契，能欣赏我们本来模样的人，C项是原文 相关内容的同义转述，故选C。A，B两项的内容原文没有提及，故排除。最后一段提到，当我们鼓起勇气 展现真实自我时，并非每个人都会看见并欣赏这样的我们，但如果他们不懂得欣赏，那是他们的损失，而 非我们的，这是在鼓励人们展现真实的自我，并没有提及人们在追求内在美的时候是否会去于展现真实的 自己，D项的说法在文中没有依据，故排除。 Passage Two\nD）我们将去于展现真实的自己"},{"no":51,"part":"reading","kind":"mcq","stem":"51. What can we expect of plant-based meats?","options":{"A":"An increasing interest in their analyses.","B":"An enormous effort in their promotion.","C":"A huge boost in their consumption.","D":"A noticeable surge in their varieties."},"passage":"passage2","answer":"C","expl":"【答案】C。 【解析】第一段末预测植物肉市场到2030年将大幅增长至740亿美元，说明其消费量预期显著增加，对应 C 项。文章提到分析人士作出预测，并未说对分析本身的兴趣增加，因此 A 不成立，故选 C。"},{"no":52,"part":"reading","kind":"mcq","stem":"52. What do we learn about consumers buying Beyond Burger and Impossible Foods products?","options":{"A":"They are mostly non-vegetarians.","B":"They are mostly animal-rights advocates.","C":"Most of them refrain from eating meat for health reasons.","D":"Most of them eat meatless meat to protect the environment."},"passage":"passage2","answer":"A","expl":"【答案】A。 【解析】定位第二段末句。原文指出购买两家植物肉产品的人中超过九成也吃肉，因此他们大多不是素食者，对应 A 项。健康、动物权益和环境是部分人减少肉类摄入的动机，原文未说大多数购买者都出于环保，故不选 D。"},{"no":53,"part":"reading","kind":"mcq","stem":"53. What can we conclude about Impossible Foods’ products from Joan Solsman’s remark?","options":{"A":"They more often appeal to meat-eaters.","B":"They very much resemble animal meat.","C":"Some have become its signature dishes.","D":"Most of them are sold as true fake meat."},"passage":"passage2","answer":"B","expl":"【答案】B。 【解析】定位 Joan Solsman 试吃植物肉的段落。她作为长期素食者因产品过于像真肉而感到难以下咽，说明产品非常接近动物肉，对应 B 项。文中没有说它们成为公司的招牌菜，故 C 不成立，答案为 B。"},{"no":54,"part":"reading","kind":"mcq","stem":"54. Why can the habit of eating a plant-based diet lead to better health?","options":{"A":"It frees people from any known link to cancer.","B":"It provides all the nutrients for staying healthy.","C":"It helps maintain normal blood pressure and blood sugar.","D":"It reduces various health risks posed by meat consumption."},"passage":"passage2","answer":"D","expl":"【答案】D。 【解析】定位第四、五段。原文比较肉类摄入的健康风险：红肉与癌症相关，不吃肉者的血压、血糖等指标较低；概括起来，植物性饮食减少了肉类带来的多种健康风险，对应 D 项。原文没有说植物肉提供保持健康所需的所有营养素，因此 B 过于绝对，故选 D。"},{"no":55,"part":"reading","kind":"mcq","stem":"55. What does the author recommend we eat at the end of the passage?","options":{"A":"Naturally produced foods.","B":"Processed protein substitutes.","C":"Red meats like traditional beef.","D":"New generations of alternate meat."},"passage":"passage2","answer":"A","expl":"在文章结尾，作者建议我们吃什么？\nA）天然食物。\nB）加工过的蛋白质替代品 根据关键词attheendofthepassage定位到最后一段。 最后一段作者指出，这些新一代替代肉类备受关注，但也别忘了全谷物和蔬菜。作者还指出，豆类是极佳 的蛋白质替代品，它们营养丰富，价格实惠。这些都说明作者建议人们吃天然食物，故选A。作者批评加 工素肉钠含量高，且建议用天然豆类替代，故排除B项。作者指出了红肉的健康风险，明确反对红肉，故 排除C项。作者认为新一代替代肉类仍是加工品，应优先选择天然食物，故排除D项。 四载2025.6第三营47 Part IV\nC）像传统牛肉这样的红肉。\nD）新一代的替代肉类"}],"warnings":[]};